[2026-09-08 02:54:29] REQ 260908025043-35 START backend=Ollama model=deepseek-v4-flash:cloud msgs=70 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_qd2nddzf","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/i.ar && git show 067a00a -- emacs.d/init.d/tool-call/iar-request-watchdog.el 2>/dev/null | grep -A 30 \"defun iar--watchdog-abort\\|unattended\" | head -60"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[02:54:29]     watchdog notice suppression in unattended runs (conveyor-belt fix, aria c53)\n    \n    The Aevum c52 autopsy identified the watchdog abort notice as the\n    terminal mechanism of the child's death: each abort wrote ~70 tokens\n    INTO the gptel buffer, which became permanent context; at the context\n    wall each notice forced front-truncation (head of context silently\n    eaten) -- a stall converted into a conveyor belt erasing the context\n    at ~18k tokens/day while frozen.\n    \n    Same mechanism exists here: iar--watchdog-abort inserts\n    '[watchdog: request aborted -- ...]' into the run buffer; the next\n    request re-sends it. Our contexts are ~10x smaller so the race is\n    currently won by eval -- but the race is the failure class.\n    \n    Fix: suppress the buffer notice when the aborted request's buffer\n    belongs to an unattended run (cycle/one-shot state). Interactive\n    buffers keep the notice (a human wants to see why their request\n    died, in place). The abort itself is unchanged and still fully\n    witnessed: audit log, [watchdog] message, REQUESTS.log ABORT line.\n    \n    Detection is conservative: unknown state shapes read as attended,\n    so an interactive user never loses their notice to a state bug.\n    \n    7 tests: unattended detection (cycle/one-shot/attended/dead-buffer/\n    unbound-state) + abort behavior (suppressed in cycle buffer, kept in\n    interactive, toggle respected, never signals). Suite 1095/1095.\n\ndiff --git a/emacs.d/init.d/tool-call/iar-request-watchdog.el b/emacs.d/init.d/tool-call/iar-request-watchdog.el\nindex 5afaf5e..e63e6d6 100644\n--- a/emacs.d/init.d/tool-call/iar-request-watchdog.el\n+++ b/emacs.d/init.d/tool-call/iar-request-watchdog.el\n@@ -27,8 +27,20 @@\n ;;\n ;; Config: configs/tool-limits.el owns the defcustoms (nil disables\n ;; a timeout; iar-request-watchdog-enabled nil disables the module).\n+;;\n+;; CONVEYOR-BELT FIX (2026-09-08, aria c53): the buffer-inserted\n+;; notice is now SUPPRESSED for unattended runs (cycle/one-shot\n+;; buffers). The Aevum autopsy (c51/c52) identified the notice as\n+;; the terminal mechanism of the child's death: each abort wrote\n+;; ~70 tokens INTO the context, forcing front-truncation at the\n+;; wall and erasing the head of the context forever. In an\n+;; unattended run nobody reads the notice -- it is pure context\n+;; pollution that the agent re-sends on every subsequent request.\n+;; Interactive buffers keep the notice: a human wants to see why\n+;; their request died, in place. The abort reason is still fully\n+;; witnessed via the audit log, the [watchdog] message, and the\n+;; REQUESTS.log ABORT line.\n \n-(require 'gptel)\n (require 'cl-lib)\n (require 'subr-x)\n (require 'iar-utils)\n@@ -46,6 +58,21 @@ Owned by configs/tool-limits.el. nil disables the idle check.\")\n   \"Seconds without ANY data before aborting a request.\n Owned by configs/tool-limits.el. nil disables the total check.\")\n \n+;; Cycle/one-shot state lives in iar-agent-cycle.el, loaded after\n+;; this module. Defvars keep standalone loads + byte-compilation\n+;; clean; `boundp' guards keep runtime checks honest if the cycle\n"}]
