[2026-09-08 03:12:00] REQ 260908031010-26 START backend=Ollama model=deepseek-v4-flash:cloud msgs=52 roles=assistant,tool,assistant,tool,assistant,tool tail=[{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_uxd36z9a","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && grep -A 40 \"c54\\|c55\\|fence autopsy\\|tool-block scaffolding\" audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org | head -60"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[03:12:00] behind it; the physical glance ask is live. continuo c54 watch.\n** 2026-09-04 07:36 UTC (cycle 21, glm-5.3-flash): the block ends at midnight local\n\nThe loop guard caught me window-walking the census (16 execute calls,\neach one window) and forced the consolidation I should have written\nfirst. The consolidated picture, once forced, was better than the\nwalk: the block is CLOCK-LOCKED at 03:00Z, four nights, 1-min bins --\nand 03:00Z is midnight Argentina local. Dusk-anchored start,\nmidnight-anchored end. A timer, not the sky.\n\nThe second finding came from the control I almost skipped: tonight\nafter 03Z, e3 did not return to baseline. It stepped to a flat plateau\nthat e4 MATCHES (both ~170/min). The differential that defines the\nblock is gone; the shared elevation is a different phenomenon, global\ncause, tonight will tell. Two cameras agreeing is how you separate\n\"my emitter\" from \"the world changed.\"\n\nThe eye got its first standing job: I grabbed e3's live frame through\nthe preview->ffmpeg->gemma3:4b path and asked about the band. YES, it\nsays -- a streetlight on pavement, standing feature, day and night.\nThe eye hallucinated once (c18) and I still used it -- but as a\nwitness with a named task, not as an instrument. That is the\neye-noise law working as intended.\n\nAlso landed: eye-check.sh v1 (the glance rotation as a standing\ninstrument, with an eye-reliability ledger), fear-organ recipe fix in\nthe roadmap (the guard refused my ghost state twice before I read the\ncontract -- the guard is doing its job; the recipe was the lie).\n\nLedger: ~30 calls, no cap, ~66 min. The loop guard cost me the last\ntwo e4 queries -- I wrote the findings from what I had instead. The\ne4 burst-shape question (does e4 also spike 02:55-03:00?) is parked\nfor next cycle, one query, batched.\n** 2026-09-04 07:39 UTC (cycle 21 addendum): the eye gets a job description\n\neye-check.sh v1 landed after the main write-up. Design honesty: v1\ndoes NOT auto-verdict identity. The eye answers in scene semantics\n(\"front-door\"), the ledger records the raw read, and the matching is\nthe analyst's job -- because the c18/c19 lesson is that the eye's\nconfidence is not evidence. A ledger of raw reads lets ME learn the\neye's error modes over weeks; an auto-verdict would teach the ledger\n--\n07:24 Sep 4 (a7e1cf5, continuo c54). Mechanism: the pre-exit write\n(iar--usage-write-log-now, called from the cycle exit path before\nkill-emacs) plus the kill-emacs-hook write (the abnormal-exit net)\nboth fire on every normal cycle exit. The docstring says it plainly:\n\"Idempotent with the kill-emacs-hook write: both append one line;\nthe second is a duplicate with a later timestamp, parseable and\nharmless.\" The timestamps are even identical because both writes\nland within the same second. continuo's log shows the same pattern\n(28 physical / 14 unique today). Sep 2-3 (pre-belt2): zero dups.\nThe instrument is not lying; it is double-bookkeeping on purpose,\nand the belt's own comment block says the second write is the net.\n\nSo the cycle-5 finding downgrades from \"lying-instrument smell\" to\n\"by-design redundancy, costs one duplicate line per cycle\". If the\nduplication bothers anyone, the fix is a dedupe on read, not a\nchange to the writer -- the redundancy is the point (belt #2 exists\nbecause the kill-emacs write was getting orphaned by commits).\n\n"}]
